@@ -133,3 +133,4 @@ _Maintained by Claude Code_
 | 2026-05-02 | **Homepage redesign — Claude Design handoff `T3xTdERm9wlM8SFyWYpaJw` shipped to `main`** | Three structural changes landed against the marketing homepage. **Hero** rebuilt as "loud sales-mode version C": red `Family-Owned · Temple, TX` eyebrow → Barlow `Welded right. / Up by Friday.` headline with brand-blue accent on the promise word → generic `Welded · Central Texas` verified chip top-right (no fabricated build dates) → 4-up form-chip ribbon (Carport / Garage / Barn / Lean-to) linking to `/#quote` → "Scroll for the full quote" cue → 4-up proof strip (`150+ Central TX builds / 24h Quote reply / ★★★★★ Local on Google / In-house Welders & installers`) sharing the same dark ground. **Services** refactored to Editorial Split treatment (option C in the design's `components-card-photo.html`): photo on top with no overlay text, brand-700 magazine eyebrow, Barlow display headline, body, hairline rule capping a `From $X · steel + install` + `See builds →` row. Section ground switched to warm paper `#fbf9f5`, grid 4-up at lg. **Header** got a 1-line tweak — `-mt-1` on the lion image to optically align the lion's bottom-weighted face with the cap-line of "Triple". **TrustBar** dropped from homepage (proof strip absorbs it); component file retained for service-detail / location pages. Three new CSS gradient utilities added to `globals.css` (`.hero-scrim`, `.hero-bottom-fade`, `.hero-chip-overlay`) since 4-stop gradients can't be expressed in Tailwind's `from/via/to`. Out of scope: WhyTripleJ, HowItWorks, Gallery, Testimonials, ServiceAreas, QuoteForm, Footer (design transcript explicitly preserved them). Commits: `207f35c` (homepage redesign) + `5560e74` (services pricing realignment). |
 | 2026-05-02 | **Homepage Services pricing realigned to Marketplace anchors + steel-install framing** | First pass of the homepage redesign listed Garage from $9,400 (extrapolated 20×20 + 4 walls + roll-up door). That silently bundled walls + door — exactly the misrepresentation the 2026-05-02 unbundling sweep was correcting on Marketplace. Followed up immediately: Garage card now reads `From $5,500 · steel + install` (matches Marketplace listing B's 30×30 shell anchor), with headline + blurb rewritten to make the shell-only scope explicit ("Enclosed shop space — your spec, our crew. 30×30 welded shell starts here. Walls, roll-up doors, walk-throughs, and insulation are quoted on top per your spec."). All four cards now use the consistent `From $X · steel + install` meta line: Carports $3,000 / Garages $5,500 / Barns $6,500 / RV & Boat $3,800. Cross-channel pricing parity with Marketplace is now intact — a buyer arriving from listing B sees the same anchor on the homepage. Concrete, walls, doors, and windows remain quoted on top per spec. Memory: `feedback_ad_pricing_accuracy.md` Rule 3 (steel + install only) governs all four cards. |
 | 2026-05-02 | **Turnkey-as-offering vs. turnkey-as-price-label clarified — over-correction reverted** | Mid-session I read Julian's 2026-05-02 unbundling sweep too aggressively and stripped "turnkey" from the homepage metadata + Services section lede (commit `d3a7cd6`). Julian flagged: turnkey IS a real Triple J offering (single contract: site prep + concrete + structure), the only problem is attaching the steel+install floor prices ($3,000 / $5,500 / $6,500 / $3,800) to the word "turnkey" — those prices are shell-only. The fix: keep "turnkey" wherever it describes the service offering with no specific price attached (metadata, About, Military, blog posts, schema.org descriptions, the `/services/turnkey-carports-with-concrete` route slug, SEO keyword arrays); only police the cases where a shell-only price is being labeled "turnkey." Reverted `d3a7cd6` (`2cf5250`). The Services *cards* keep the "From $X · steel + install" framing from `5560e74` because those numbers ARE the steel+install floors. New durable rule logged to memory `feedback_turnkey_vs_steel_install.md`. |
+| 2026-05-06 | **Roll-up door price list LOCKED from Freddy/Juan handwritten sheet** | Julian shared the office price sheet for roll-up doors and asked to lock it in. Canonical installed prices: **8×8 = $1,000 · 10×10 = $1,900 · 12×12 = $1,800 · 14×14 = $2,200 · 16×16 = $2,600**. Replaces the placeholder ladder in `src/lib/quote-pricing.ts` (`9x8 850 / 10x10 1100 / 12x12 1450 / 14x14 1900 / 16x14 2200`). `RollupSize` type updated to `'8x8' \| '10x10' \| '12x12' \| '14x14' \| '16x16'` — `9x8` and `16x14` dropped (not stocked on the canonical sheet); 8×8 and 16×16 added. `CalculatorStep.tsx` `ROLLUP_SIZES` array updated to match. **Anomaly flagged:** 10×10 ($1,900) is priced ABOVE 12×12 ($1,800) on the sheet — preserved as written but called out in a code comment + here for Julian's next price-sheet pass; if it's a transcription slip, swap 10×10 to ~$1,400 (likely intended) on the next pass. Done on branch `claude/quote-rollup-door-pricing-jB54p`. |
